"Emendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus (https://scispace.com/pdf/emendation-of-bacteroidaceae-and-butyrivibrio-and-3spebb4661.pdf)\nciteturn61view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn57view1\",\"lineno\":36}); Total lines: 1449\nL16@P0: lactaris, R. obeum, Eubacterium dolichum, E. hadrum, E. ramulus, E. sir\u0002aeum, and Clostridium leptum, are described. These species have been isolated\nL17@P0: from human intestinal contents or from human feces. The type strains of these\nL18@P0: species are ATCC zyxwvuts\nL19@P0: 29098, 29175, 29149, 29176, 29174, 29143, 29173, 29099, 29066,\nL20@P0: and 29065, respectively.\nL21@P0: During studies of the bacteria from the feces\nL22@P0: and the contents of the gastrointestinal tracts\nL23@P0: of humans, cultures of several previously un\u0002described species that are predominant among\nL24@P0: the bacteria in the intestines of some individ\u0002uals were isolated. Species for which we have\nL25@P0: 10 or more isolates from six or more individuals\nL26@P0: are described below. These isolates were from\nL27@P0: studies of 177 specimens from 90 individuals.\nL28@P0: Discussion of the incidence of most of these\nL29@P0: species, as compared with other kinds of orga\u0002nisms of the intestinal flora, is published else\u0002where (3, 9).\nL30@P0: MATERIALS AND METHODS\nL31@P0: The organisms described in this report were iso\u0002lated and characterized according to anaerobic-tube\nL32@P0: culture methods and procedures published previ\u0002ously (4, 5, 9, 11, 12). Single colonies were picked\nL33@P0: from rumen fluid-glucose-cellobiose agar (RGCA)\nL34@P0: roll tubes, inoculated with dilutions of feces or\nL35@P0: intestinal contents, after incubation for 5 days\nL36@P0: under oxygen-free carbon dioxide. After one or more\nL37@P0: streaking and picking series, to verify a pure cul\u0002ture, fermentative and biochemical characteristics\nL38@P0: in prereduced media zyxwvuts(5) were determined.\nL39@P0: To determine hemolytic activity, cultures were\nL40@P0: streaked on freshly prepared blood agar plates\nL41@P0: (BAP) of brain heart infusion agar supplemented\nL42@P0: (per 100 ml) with 0.5 g of yeast extract, 0.1 p1 of\nL43@P0: vitamin K1, 0.5 mg of hemin (BHIA-S), and 5.0 ml of\nL44@P0: defibrinated sheep blood. Inoculated BAP were in\u0002cubated 40 to zyxwvuts\nL45@P0: 48 h in a vented GasPak (BBL) anaer\u0002obe jar with a gaseous atmosphere of 90% H,-10%\nL46@P0: CO,. Reactions on egg yolk agar (EYA) medium (5)\nL47@P0: were determined after similar incubation.\nL48@P0: The moles percent guanine plus cytosine (G+C)\nL49@P0: contents of the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) prepa\u0002rations were determined by the thermal melting\nL50@P0: point (T,) method (8) by using an automatic record\u0002ing spectrophotometer (Gilford Instrument Labora\u0002tories). DNA fromEscherichia coli B was included in\nL51@P0: each set of analyses as a standard.\nL52@P0: RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\nL53@P0: Members of the following new taxa were iso\u0002lated fkom the intestinal contents or feces of\nL54@P0: humans.\nL55@P0: Desulfomonas gem nov. and D. pigra sp.\nL56@P0: nov. Desulfomonus gen. nov. (De.suZ.fo.mo'nas.\nL57@P0: L. pref. de from; L. n. sulfur sulfur; Gr. fem. n.\nL58@P0: moms unit; M. L. fem. n. Desulfomonas a cell\nL59@P0: that reduces sulfir compounds). This genus in\u0002cludes the obligately anaerobic, straight, non\u0002motile, gram-negative, nonsporeforming, sul\u0002fate-reducing rods. The metabolic activity, uti\u0002lization of pyruvate to produce acetate, reduc\u0002tion of sulfate, presence of cytochrome, and\nL60@P0: relatively high moles percent G+C content of\nL61@P0: the DNA, indicate that the genus is probably\nL62@P0: phylogenetically related to Desulfovibrio and\nL63@P0: Desulfotomaculum. However, those genera are\nL64@P0: classified according to morphological proper\u0002ties (cell shape and polar flagella, or produc\u0002tion of spores) not exhibited by the presently\nL65@P0: described organisms. Therefore, we place the\nL66@P0: genus Desulfomonas in the family Bacteroi\u0002daceae pending more information that might\nL67@P0: relate the three morphologically distinct, sul\u0002fate-reducing genera in a single higher taxon,\nL68@P0: possibly at the family level.\nL69@P0: DesuZfomonas pigra sp. nov. (L. adj. pig'ra\nL70@P0-1: 238 VOL. 26, zyxwvutsrqp\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus (https://scispace.com/pdf/emendation-of-bacteroidaceae-and-butyrivibrio-and-3spebb4661.pdf)\nciteturn61view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn57view1\",\"lineno\":385}); Total lines: 1449\nL363@P3: the organism in the same genus with B. fibri\u0002soluens. We do not believe that the number of\nL364@P3: polar flagella is a sufficiently important charac\u0002teristic to warrant the creation of a new genus,\nL365@P3: particularly since individual strains of bacteria\nL366@P3: with polar flagella vary in respect to numbers\nL367@P3-4: of flagella. Therefore, we propose an emended 242 MOORE, JOHNSON, AND HOLDEMAN zyxwvu\nL368@P4: INT. J. SYST. BACTERIOL.\nL369@P4: photrichous cells. See\nL370@P4: FIG. 2. zyxwvutsrqpoButyrivibrio\nL371@P4: Fig.\nL372@P4: crossotus\nL373@P4: ll for scale.\nL374@P4: ATCC 291 75. Lo- zyxwvut\nL375@P4: description of the genus Butyrivibrio to include\nL376@P4: curved rods which occur singly or in* chains or\nL377@P4: filaments and which may or may not be helical.\nL378@P4: Cells are motile, monotrichous or lophotri\u0002chous, with polar or subpolar flagella; nonmo\u0002tile variants may occur. No resting stages are\nL379@P4: known. Cells are gram negative. Metabolism is\nL380@P4: fermentative, carbohydrates being the main\nL381@P4: fermentable substrates, with the production of\nL382@P4: butyrate as one of the important products.\nL383@P4: Strains are strictly anaerobic.\nL384@P4: Butyrivibrio crossotus sp. nov. (Gr. adj.\nL385@P4: cross. so’tus tasseled [referring to the multiple\nL386@P4: polar or subpolar flagella]). Cells are obligately\nL387@P4: anaerobic, lophotrichous (polar or subpolar),\nL388@P4: nonsporeforming, gram-negative rods with\nL389@P4: rounded to slightly tapered ends. Cells were\nL390@P4: usually curved, at least soon after isolation\nL391@P4: from the natural habitat, and occurred singly\nL392@P4: or in pairs and short chains (Fig. 2 and 3). Pairs\nL393@P4: were often in an “S” arrangement. From PYG\nL394@P4: broth cultures, cells were 0.5 to 0.7 pm wide\nL395@P4: and 2.0 to 5.0 pm long. Three of 29 isolates\nL396@P4: tested were nonmotile, and no flagella could be\nL397@P4: seen in flagella stains from several subcultures;\nL398@P4: however, they had the other characteristics\nL399@P4: typical for this species, and they are probably\nL400@P4: nonmotile variants. However, it is also possible\nL401@P4: that the flagella were broken off during prepa\u0002ration of the suspension for flagella staining.\nL402@P4: Colonies. After incubation for 5 days in\nL403@P4: RGCA roll tubes, subsurface colonies were 0.5\nL404@P4: to 1.0 mm in diameter, lenticular, and translu\u0002cent\nL405@P4: S\nL406@P4: circular,\nL407@P4: roll\nL408@P4: to\nL409@P4: streaks\nL410@P4: transparent.\nL411@P4: entire,\nL412@P4: were\nL413@P4: convex,\nL414@P4: 0.2\nL415@P4: Surface\nL416@P4: to\nL417@P4: translucent\nL418@P4: 1.0\nL419@P4: colonies\nL420@P4: mm in diameter,\nL421@P4: on\nL422@P4: to\nL423@P4: BHIA\u0002semi- zyxwv\nL424@P4: opaque, and smooth. Only 3 of the 10 strains\nL425@P4: tested grew as surface colonies on BAP incu\u0002bated anaerobically; surface colonies were the\nL426@P4: same as on the BHIA-S roll streaks, and no\nL427@P4: hemolytic activity was observed. The few\nL428@P4: strains that grew on EYA produced no lecithin\u0002ase or lipase reaction.\nL429@P4: Cultural characteristic‘s. There was poor\nL430@P4: growth in PY broth that did not contain a fer\u0002mentable carbohydrate. PY-maltose cultures\nL431@P4: had abundant growth with smooth, flocculent,\nL432@P4: or ropy sediment; there usually was some tur\u0002bidity.\nL433@P4: The optimum temperature for growth was\nL434@P4: 37 C. Some strains grew equally well at 45 C.\nL435@P4: There was either no growth or very poor growth\nL436@P4: at 30 C.\nL437@P4: Biochemical reactions. Some of the biochemi\u0002cal characteristics of 31 isolates of this species\nL438@P4: are given in Table 1. In addition, the type\nL439@P4: strain and nine other strains tested did not\nL440@P4: grow well in, or ferment, adonitol, galactose,\nL441@P4: glycerol, inulin, or sorbose. All produced acid in\nL442@P4: PY-dextrin. There was little or no growth in PY\nL443@P4: media containing lactate, pyruvate, or threo\u0002nine. No ammonia was detected from PY or CM\nL444@P4: cultures (with Nessler reagent).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus (https://scispace.com/pdf/emendation-of-bacteroidaceae-and-butyrivibrio-and-3spebb4661.pdf)\nciteturn61view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn57view1\",\"lineno\":1400}); Total lines: 1449\nL1376@P13: Clostridium. Phenotypically, it is unlike any\nL1377@P13: other described species in the genus. It most\nL1378@P13: closely resembles E. siraeum, from which it\nL1379@P13: differs in G+C content of the DNA, production\nL1380@P13: of spores, and lack of cellobiose fermentation.\nL1381@P13: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nL1382@P13: We gratefully acknowledge the support of this work by\nL1383@P13: the Public Health Service (National Institute of General\nL1384@P13: Medical Sciences grant 14604; National Cancer Institute\nL1385@P13: contract N01-CP-33334) and by the National Aeronautics\nL1386@P13: and Space Administration (contract NASA 9-12601).\nL1387@P13: We are indebted to Thomas 0. MacAdoo of the Foreign\nL1388@P13: Language Department of this institution for providing ap\u0002propriate specific epithets for the species described. We\nL1389@P13: especially appreciate the technical assistance of Loretta\nL1390@P13: Albert, Roger Cook, Maeve Crowgey, Pauletta Cumbee,\nL1391@P13: Arm Donnelly, Linda Hoffman, Dan Linn, Steven Lowe,\nL1392@P13: Carol Phelps, Christina Spittle, Barbara Thompson,\nL1393@P13: DiannP Wall, and others of our laboratory who performed\nL1394@P13: work on or for this project. We thank Elizabeth P. Cat0 for\nL1395@P13: help in compilation of the data, and M. P. Bryant for\nL1396@P13: suggestions concerning analysis of Desulfornonas pigra .\nL1397@P13: REPRINT REQUESTS\nL1398@P13: Address reprint requests to: W. E. C. Moore, Anaerobe\nL1399@P13: Laboratory, Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State Uni\u0002versity, P.O. Box 49, Blacksburg, Va. 24060.\nL1400@P13: LITEKATUKE CITED\nL1401@P13: 1. Bryant, M. P. 1974. GenusButyriuibrio, p. 420. In R. E.\nL1402@P13: Buchanan, and N. E. Gibbons (ed.), Bergey’s manual\nL1403@P13: of determinative bacteriology, The Williams and Wil\u0002kins Co., Baltimore.\nL1404@P13: 2. Bryant, M. zyxwvP., and N. Small. 1956. The anaerobic mon\u0002otrichous butyric acid-producing curved rod-shaped\nL1405@P13: bacteria of the rumen. J. Bacteriol. 72:16-21.\nL1406@P13: 3. Holdeman, L. V., I. J. Good, and W. E. C. Moore. 1976.\nL